WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.436
Raw API keys should be treated like passwords or bearer credentials.

2
00:00:04.436 --> 00:00:10.306
If someone can read the raw key, they may be able to use it wherever that key is accepted.

3
00:00:10.306 --> 00:00:19.830
That is why keys do not belong in source code, ordinary appsettings files, public configuration, documentation, sample projects, or support notes.

4
00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:23.940
Development secret storage is not production secret management.

5
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:38.551
Developer tools can keep local work from becoming hardcoded configuration, but production secrets should use environment-appropriate secret stores, managed platforms, deployment identity, or controlled configuration systems.

6
00:00:38.551 --> 00:00:45.009
Production storage needs access control, rotation support, auditability, and operational ownership.

7
00:00:45.009 --> 00:00:53.946
For inbound client API keys, prefer storing a non-reversible verifier such as a strong hash, plus a separate lookup identifier or prefix.

8
00:00:53.946 --> 00:01:03.470
This lets the system identify a candidate key record and validate the presented value without retaining every full raw secret in ordinary storage.

9
00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:10.384
A key prefix or identifier is useful for lookup, display, support, and rotation, but it is not the secret.

10
00:01:10.384 --> 00:01:16.907
Prefixes should help humans and systems find the right record without granting access by themselves.

11
00:01:16.907 --> 00:01:23.887
Support tooling should display enough to identify the key record, not enough to reconstruct or use the key.

12
00:01:23.887 --> 00:01:27.344
Generated keys need enough entropy for their purpose.

13
00:01:27.344 --> 00:01:40.651
The generation process should be owned and consistent, and keys should be distinguishable from ordinary text so teams can recognize their type, environment, or issuer without exposing the secret material.

14
00:01:40.651 --> 00:01:45.087
Validation behavior should avoid unnecessary information disclosure.

15
00:01:45.087 --> 00:01:54.024
Candidate keys should be compared carefully, failures should be consistent, and logs should record safe identifiers rather than raw keys.

16
00:01:54.024 --> 00:01:58.916
The user-facing response should not reveal which part of validation failed.

17
00:01:58.916 --> 00:02:01.264
Plan for multi-instance deployments.

18
00:02:01.264 --> 00:02:11.962
Validation data, caches, revocation state, and rotation windows need to behave consistently across application instances, regions, gateways, and background workers.

19
00:02:11.962 --> 00:02:19.138
A key that is revoked in one place should not remain active elsewhere because state was cached without a plan.
